---
alwaysApply: true
---

Dependency rule:
- Layers, from outer to inner: frameworks and UI, adapters, use cases, entities
- Source code dependencies point inward only: a layer may import from inner layers, never from outer ones
- Use cases and entities never import framework or UI code, and never perform I/O directly (browser APIs, HTTP, storage)

Layers:
- Entities: core business types and rules, the code least likely to change when the UI or storage changes; no I/O
- Use cases: one use case per application action (`cancelSubscription`, `publishGif`); they orchestrate entities and reach external systems only through ports
- Adapters: wrap external systems (browser APIs, HTTP, storage) and convert data between external formats and internal formats; when a use case declares a port, the adapter implements it
- Frameworks and UI: rendering, user input, and glue code that wires adapters to use cases; keep them thin

Boundaries:
- A port is an interface that a use case declares for an external system it needs
- Pass adapters to use cases as function arguments; do not add a dependency injection library
- Entities and simple data structures may cross boundaries; database rows and framework objects (DOM events, HTTP responses) may not

Scope:
- Create use cases and ports when a feature has business logic: validation, calculations, or decisions
- When a feature only fetches and displays data, the UI may call an adapter directly
